WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.130
- [Narrator] This is the
woman behind what is arguably

00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:05.990
the most iconic theme song on television.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:10.280
("I'll Be There For
You" by The Rembrandts)

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.670
But did you know that this
woman is also responsible

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.300
for some of the most iconic songs

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.100
on the radio in the last century?

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.950
- Biggest hits would be "September,"

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:19.850
- [Narrator] OK.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:20.790
("September" by Earth, Wind &amp; Fire)

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.340
- [Allee] "What Have I
Done To Deserve This,"

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.202
- [Narrator] Oh, that's a good one!

00:00:23.202 --> 00:00:24.035
("What Have I Done To Deserve
This" by Pet Shop Boys)

00:00:24.035 --> 00:00:24.868
("Stir It Up" by Patti LaBelle)

00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:26.660
- [Allee] Stir It Up
from Beverly Hills Cop,

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:27.880
- [Narrator] That one, too?

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.613
- [Allee] "You're The
Best" from "Karate Kid,"

00:00:30.613 --> 00:00:31.446
- [Narrator] I know that one!

00:00:31.446 --> 00:00:33.340
- [Allee] The Color Purple musical.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:34.520
- [Narrator] That's a lot of songs!

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:36.610
- I've written over a thousand songs.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:37.657
- [Narrator] Jeez.

00:00:37.657 --> 00:00:40.574
(electronic music)

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.889
(birds chirping)

00:00:44.889 --> 00:00:46.870
(upbeat music)

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.530
- I'm Allee Willis.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.840
I am a Songwriters Hall of Fame,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.260
Grammy, Emmy, Tony,
and Webby award winning

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.260
and nominated songwriter, artist,

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.850
director, collector, art director,

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:06.040
social artist, party-thrower.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.093
Most important one.

00:01:08.093 --> 00:01:09.000
(upbeat music)

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.950
I don't know how to do anything.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:15.801
I don't know how to play an
instrument, read or write music.

00:01:15.801 --> 00:01:17.521
(maraca rattles)

00:01:17.521 --> 00:01:18.430
(laughing)

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:22.180
I had seen less than five musicals

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.410
before I wrote "The Color Purple."

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:27.050
I'm best when I don't know what I'm doing.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:28.480
- [Narrator] In that
case, she really must not

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.050
have known what she was
doing when she wrote

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.930
"I'll Be There For You," the
theme song for "Friends."

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.330
It went on to land her
an Emmy nomination--

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:39.750
- And we lost to the theme
from "Star Trek: Voyager."

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.383
I dare anyone to sing that now.

00:01:42.383 --> 00:01:44.640
("Star Trek: Voyager" Main Title)

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.250
It's like, really?

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:47.650
Compared to the "Friends" theme?

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.584
But, that's how it goes.

00:01:49.584 --> 00:01:50.417
("I'll Be There For
You" by The Rembrandts)

00:01:50.417 --> 00:01:51.640
- [Narrator] Nevertheless,
"I'll Be There" For You

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.240
went down in pop culture history,

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.390
just like Allee's earlier big hit,

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:57.300
"September" by Earth, Wind &amp; Fire.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.410
- Do you remember the
21st night of September?

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.400
Love was changing the minds of pretenders,

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.410
while chasing the clouds away.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:09.020
We very purposely wanted
to write a happy song,

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:13.013
and I don't think there's any
happier song than "September."

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.330
- The beginning of 1978,
I was on food stamps,

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.910
medical assistance, really
as close as you could be

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:24.380
to welfare without actually being on it.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:28.150
By the end of the year I'd
sold 10 million records.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:32.633
It was a surreal experience,
which my entire life is, so.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.200
If I wasn't allowed to be creative,

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.730
I would shrivel up and die, I think.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.960
Open the coffin, when
you throw me in there,

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:45.580
that's when I stop.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:49.260
Though probably I'll still
be like, banging the sides,

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.560
hearing some kind of beat.

00:02:58.087 --> 00:03:00.754
(ringing music)

